WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.100
SQL fundamentals.

2
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:05.933
Every data conversation quietly assumes you know SQL.

3
00:00:05.933 --> 00:00:10.600
One fact unlocks the whole language: the order a query really runs in.

4
00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:14.133
SQL is declarative set logic.

5
00:00:14.433 --> 00:00:15.618
You never write a loop.

6
00:00:15.618 --> 00:00:20.100
You describe the shape of the result you want, and the planner works out how to get it.

7
00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:23.784
Here is the order a query actually runs in.

8
00:00:23.784 --> 00:00:25.633
It is not the order you type it.

9
00:00:25.933 --> 00:00:27.600
First, FROM picks the tables.

10
00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:29.967
WHERE then filters individual rows.

11
00:00:30.267 --> 00:00:33.133
GROUP BY collapses each group into a single row.

12
00:00:33.433 --> 00:00:36.567
HAVING filters those groups, after the counting is done.

13
00:00:36.867 --> 00:00:40.300
Only now does SELECT build the columns and their aliases.

14
00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:42.700
And ORDER BY sorts the final result.

15
00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:45.967
This order explains the two filters.

16
00:00:46.267 --> 00:00:49.533
WHERE runs before grouping, so it only sees single rows.

17
00:00:49.833 --> 00:00:53.333
HAVING runs after aggregation, so it filters whole groups.

18
00:00:53.633 --> 00:00:56.615
That is why HAVING can see a count and WHERE cannot.

19
00:00:56.615 --> 00:00:58.967
Do as much filtering in WHERE as you can.

20
00:01:00.167 --> 00:01:01.261
Here it is in code.

21
00:01:01.261 --> 00:01:03.967
Which satellites had three or more weak passes?

22
00:01:04.267 --> 00:01:07.267
WHERE throws away strong passes first, row by row.

23
00:01:07.567 --> 00:01:11.633
Then we group by satellite, and HAVING keeps only groups with at least three.

24
00:01:11.933 --> 00:01:16.300
Finally ORDER BY can use the weak passes alias, because it runs last.

25
00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:18.967
Here is where this pays off.

26
00:01:19.267 --> 00:01:21.433
Ops asks which satellites are degrading.

27
00:01:21.733 --> 00:01:25.933
That is one GROUP BY with a HAVING, run straight on the telemetry database.

28
00:01:26.233 --> 00:01:30.067
The answer comes back in seconds, with no pandas and no export.

29
00:01:31.267 --> 00:01:32.088
Let's recap.

30
00:01:32.088 --> 00:01:33.390
SQL is declarative.

31
00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:35.033
You describe the result.

32
00:01:35.333 --> 00:01:37.800
Clauses run in a fixed logical order.

33
00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:40.800
WHERE filters rows, and HAVING filters groups.

34
00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:44.380
And aliases only exist after SELECT runs.

35
00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:45.900
Next up: SQL joins.
